INT. BOWLING ALLEY LEAGUE NIGHT - NIGHT

Lane 7. Scoreboard flickering. Beer pitchers sweating. Pins crash somewhere behind.

Four people at a table bolted to the floor: two couples who used to be friends.

RUSSELL KOWALCZYK, 40s, wears a team shirt that reads "Pin Pals." He keeps score by hand. Pencil only.

DARLENE OSEI-WRIGHT, 40s, his wife, nurses a ginger ale. She hasn't looked at the scoreboard.

MARIANNE TOUSSAINT, 40s, sits very straight, purse in lap. She is waiting for something.

BEAUFORT "BO" TOUSSAINT, 40s, her husband, lifts the ball from the return and weighs it.

BO
Your frame, Russ.

RUSSELL
Yep.

He doesn't rise. Marianne glances at Darlene. Darlene studies the carpet.

BO
Russ?

RUSSELL
I'm thinking.

DARLENE
He's always thinking when he's winning.

RUSSELL
He's not winning.

MARIANNE
Who isn't?

RUSSELL
Nobody. Forget it.

The ball clacks back down the return. Bo catches it. He holds it like a small planet.

BO
Eleven pins left standing from the last. Somebody's got to knock them down.

Marianne stands. Smooths her skirt.

MARIANNE
I need air.

DARLENE
I'll come.

MARIANNE
No.

Darlene sits. Russell's pencil taps the table. Tap. Tap. Tap.

BO
That's a lot of noise for a quiet man.

RUSSELL
That's a lot of talk for a man who never says anything.

BO
Say what you mean.

RUSSELL
I'll say it on the lane.

He rises, takes the ball from Bo, and walks to the line. Bo follows two steps behind. Darlene half-stands.

Russell throws. Pins explode.

Strike.

He turns. Bo's hand is already on his collar. Russell shoves him. Bo shoves back. The table rattles. Pitchers slide. Darlene grabs a glass and ends up with it in her fist, shaking.

DARLENE
Stop it. Stop it!

The two men freeze, chest to chest. Marianne appears in the doorway, coat on, keys in hand. She looks at all three.

Nobody speaks. Beyond them, the pinsetter lowers its rack, and the ten pins stand back up.
